 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server authorizes each command, assigns each committed edit one event revision, and publishes that revision. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board and receive published revisions over a WebSocket connection to the board's own server. No realtime vendor sits in the delivery path.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+A browser that reconnects requests every revision after the last one it applied and applies the replayed revisions in order before live updates resume. The change store keeps seven days of revisions to serve that replay. While a browser's socket is not live, the coordinator sees that state, so a dropped connection is never mistaken for a quiet board.
+
+Self-hosted sockets give browsers updates well inside the 15-second freshness goal without recurring realtime-service fees or provider-specific client code. The cost is that the project owns connection capacity, reconnect behavior, and socket operations.
 
